import json, hashlib
from pathlib import Path
from datetime import datetime, timezone
P=Path('batches/glossary_batch_python_fastapi_backend_developer')
# Authored teaching situations and reasoning, indexed in frozen input order.
# Each tuple contains situation, worked explanation, misconception, and visual choice.
D={}
def add(i, intro, example, misconception, visual='HIGH:annotated_code'):
    D[i]=(intro,example,misconception,visual)

add(0,
'A browser page and an API can be hosted at different addresses. Before blaming a rejected browser interaction on a bad login, separate two questions: are the addresses same-origin, and does the API permit this caller?',
'Compare hypothetical URLs https://shop.example:443/orders and https://shop.example:443/items. Their paths differ, but their scheme, host and port match, so they share an origin. Changing the second host to api.example makes it cross-origin. Changing only the port to 8443 also makes it cross-origin. Browser permission to read the API response and endpoint permission to access the requested information remain separate checks. A browser access restriction is not a substitute for the latter.',
'Do not equate a browser-origin check with authentication. Even when browser access is permitted, the API must independently check access.', 'LOW:data_table_demo')
add(1,
'An orders service needs operations for reading an order, processing a new submission, replacing a known order and removing it. Choosing a method means choosing intended effects, not merely selecting a convenient label.',
'Picture an order at /orders/42. A GET retrieves its representation; HEAD asks for the same semantics without response content. Neither requests a change to the order. A log entry produced during retrieval is incidental, so it does not contradict safety. Now imagine sending the same replacement with PUT twice. The intended state after the second replacement is the same as after the first: that is idempotency, even though the returned responses may differ. DELETE similarly need not return identical responses on repetition to remain idempotent. In contrast, a POST asking the collection to process a new order is not generally idempotent. Work through each proposed operation with two separate questions: does it request a state change, and would identical repetition have the same intended effect? This separates safe retrieval from idempotent changes without treating them as the same property.',
'Safe does not mean absolutely no side effects, and idempotent does not mean identical responses. PUT and DELETE illustrate why an idempotent operation can still be unsafe.', 'HIGH:whiteboard_drawing')
add(2,
'A client sends an order as JSON and wants a JSON response. The outgoing body format, the response preference and the format actually returned are three different pieces of information.',
'For this exchange, Content-Type: application/json describes the submitted JSON content. Accept: application/json states the acceptable response media type. When examining the reply, inspect its own Content-Type rather than assuming the request header already established the response format. Think of Content-Type as a label attached to content and Accept as a preference sent by the client. Reading those labels separately makes it easier to describe what was sent and what came back.',
'Content-Type is not the client\'s preferred response format; Accept carries that preference.', 'LOW:data_table_demo')
add(3,
'Before writing a service function, describe the values it accepts and returns. A reader should be able to tell whether an order note can be missing as a value, and whether the caller may omit the argument altogether.',
'Consider a parameter written note: str | None. It admits a string or None, but without a default the caller must still supply an argument. Writing note: str | None = None adds permission to omit it. These are different decisions: the type describes permitted values, while the default describes what happens when the argument is not provided. For an order summary, list[str] can describe item names, set[str] can describe tags, tuple[int, str] can describe a two-part identifier/name value, and dict[str, int] can describe counts keyed by name. Read each annotation aloud rather than treating the brackets as decoration. The return annotation after -> describes the function result in the same way. None of these descriptions, by themselves, proves that actual runtime values have been checked. Keep the interface description separate from a future validation mechanism.',
'Adding a type hint does not add runtime validation. Also, allowing None is not the same as allowing an argument to be omitted.', 'MEDIUM:annotated_code')
add(4,
'Two order drafts need independent state. A class gives them a shared definition of behavior, but the two instances must not accidentally share a mutable collection intended to belong to just one draft.',
'Imagine Draft is the class and draft_a and draft_b are two instances created by calling it twice. Its __init__ method assigns an item collection to self.items. Because initialization creates a fresh collection on each self, adding an item to draft_a belongs to that instance\'s state rather than to a shared class collection. Compare that with placing one mutable items value on the class: both instances can then see the shared value. Follow the ownership, not just the attribute name. The class defines the methods, and a method receives the particular instance as its first argument when operating on that instance. A useful reasoning exercise is to draw two instance boxes and write the intended state inside each. If both boxes point to one class-level collection despite requiring independence, the model does not match the requirement.',
'Having two instances does not make every attribute independent. A mutable class variable can still be shared; create fresh instance state inside __init__ when independence is required.')
add(5,
'A service-related script reads a file, but the work inside that reading step might fail. The resource boundary should describe cleanup on both the successful path and the exceptional path.',
'In with open(...) as file, the indented block is the managed scope. Trace entry first: the manager enters successfully and supplies the resource used by the block. Trace normal exit next: leaving the block closes the file. Finally trace an exception raised while the block is using it: the managed exit still closes the file. That is the cleanup guarantee to rely on, conditional on successful entry. For a general manager, __exit__ also receives information about an exception and may suppress it by returning a true value. Therefore ask two questions separately: was the resource released, and was the failure propagated? Releasing a file does not itself mean that the failed work succeeded or that the exception was suppressed. Keep the useful work inside the indented resource boundary so that the scope is visible to the reader.',
'Cleanup and exception suppression are separate behaviors. Successful entry guarantees an exit call, not that exceptions disappear.')
add(6,
'An asynchronous service task can contain several awaits without letting another task run at every one. To reason about concurrency, identify actual suspension rather than simply counting await keywords.',
'Follow task A through an immediately completing coroutine and then through await asyncio.sleep(0). Calling the async function first creates a coroutine; awaiting it runs that coroutine and obtains its result. If that work completes immediately, A can continue without switching tasks. The sleep is different: even a zero delay suspends A, giving another runnable task an opportunity to execute. While A is suspended, the event loop still runs one task at a time; cooperative scheduling is not several tasks executing simultaneously in that loop. When the future A was waiting for completes, A resumes its coroutine. On paper, mark each operation as either completing immediately or waiting on a pending future, then mark only the latter as a possible handover, with sleep explicitly treated as suspension. This produces a more accurate execution trace than drawing an automatic switch after every await.',
'An await is not proof that another task ran. Immediately completing awaited work can return without suspension; asyncio.sleep suspends even with delay zero.', 'HIGH:whiteboard_drawing')
add(7,
'You want to add behavior around a service callable without rewriting the callable itself. A decorator lets another callable receive the function and determine what its name will refer to afterward.',
'Read @decorate above a function definition as roughly function = decorate(function). The decorator receives the function object, not the result of running that function with service data. A wrapper decorator can define an inner function that calls the supplied function and then return that inner function. The name now refers to what the decorator returned. Trace this in two stages: definition time applies the decorator and establishes the binding; a later call uses that bound value. If the returned value is the wrapper, the later call goes through the wrapper to the original callable. This distinction matters when reading service registration code: seeing @ above def means something happens while the function is defined, not just each time a request-related call is made. Keep the returned value in the trace rather than assuming decoration leaves the original binding untouched.',
'A decorator is not merely a comment attached to a function. Its returned value becomes the binding for the function name.')
add(8,
'Two backend projects may need different package versions. Installing everything into whichever interpreter happens to be on the machine makes it difficult to tell which project is using which dependencies.',
'Create .venv for one project, activate it using the command for the operating system, and then inspect the interpreter path. On Unix, which python should point into that environment; on Windows, use where python. Only after identifying the interpreter should you install a dependency with python -m pip install package. Then inspect it with python -m pip show package, checking both version and location. The location is useful evidence that this project is using its own installed package rather than another environment\'s copy. Repeat the same reasoning for the second project: its environment has its own installed packages and a particular Python installation. Activation and installation are steps in establishing that boundary; the interpreter-path check confirms which boundary your current shell commands are actually using.',
'Do not treat an activation command alone as evidence that you inspected the right interpreter. Confirm the path, and inspect package version and location.', 'HIGH:screen_demo')
add(9,
'When an HTTP request reaches a Python application, the network server and the application have different responsibilities. ASGI is the interface through which the server invokes the application and exchanges protocol events.',
'Trace one request across the boundary. The server handles the network connection and invokes the async application callable. Its scope describes the connection; receive and send are the event-exchange callables supplied to it. The application receives http.request events through receive(). To produce its reply, it sends http.response.start containing status and headers, followed by http.response.body. Draw those events as arrows rather than treating the handler\'s return value as the entire network lifecycle. The start event and body event have different jobs: one establishes response metadata and the other carries body content. Keeping the server outside the application box also clarifies who owns the network connection and who participates in the ASGI protocol. You need to understand this event boundary without implementing a server from scratch.',
'The application callable is not the component that directly owns all network handling. The ASGI server handles the connection and invokes the application through the interface.', 'HIGH:whiteboard_drawing')
add(10,
'A local login check needs to compare a submitted password with stored verification material. The stored value should not be the password itself or an encrypted value intended to be decrypted later.',
'Create a PasswordHash.recommended() instance from pwdlib. When registering a hypothetical local account, store the result of hash(password). During a later login attempt, pass the submitted plain password and stored hash to verify(plain_password, stored_hash). Salt distinguishes hashes, while the adjustable work factor increases guessing cost. These support password verification without making reversible password storage the goal. Use synthetic passwords in an exercise and keep attention on what is stored and what is supplied to the verification call.',
'Password hashing is not reversible encryption. Verification compares a submission with a stored hash rather than recovering the original password.', 'MEDIUM:annotated_code')
add(11,
'An order needs a Python representation tied to a persistent database row. ORM mapping establishes that association, including which attribute identifies the row.',
'Start with the shared declarative Base, then define an Order entity inheriting from it. Give it a __tablename__ such as orders and describe its attributes with Mapped[Type] and mapped_column(...). For the identity attribute, mapped_column(primary_key=True) marks the persistent row identity. Mentally draw one Python attribute next to its database column: the annotation and column arguments describe the mapping, while the primary-key declaration identifies rows. Do not confuse an ordinary instance having a field called id with a mapping that declares a primary key. In this exercise the goal is the entity-to-table association itself. You can inspect the declarations and explain the table name, attributes and key before moving on to later operations that retrieve or modify rows. This keeps mapping separate from performing a database write.',
'Constructing a mapped class definition does not by itself describe a completed write. Here the task is to declare the attribute-to-column association and row identity.')
add(12,
'A browser wants to make a cross-origin call using a particular method and headers. For some requests it first asks whether that intended call is permitted, before sending the actual request.',
'Trace an OPTIONS preflight for a hypothetical client origin https://shop.example that wants to use PUT with a content-type header. The preflight carries Origin and Access-Control-Request-Method, plus Access-Control-Request-Headers when requested headers need to be identified. Compare the reply\'s Access-Control-Allow-Origin, Access-Control-Allow-Methods and Access-Control-Allow-Headers with those requested values. This is a comparison between an intended call and the server\'s browser-access permission, not the actual operation\'s business response. If the cross-origin response is credentialed, look for an explicit allowed origin and Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true rather than accepting a wildcard origin. Label the preflight and actual request separately in a trace so that a successful permission negotiation is not mistaken for completion of the intended operation.',
'Not every cross-origin request is preflighted. Also, a wildcard allowed origin is not the required origin response for a credentialed exchange.', 'HIGH:whiteboard_drawing')
add(13,
'An orders API needs clients to distinguish a successful creation, an absent order and an unexpected service failure. Status codes communicate those outcomes before the client interprets the body.',
'Build an outcome table for a hypothetical order workflow. Successful retrieval can use 200; successful creation can use 201; successful completion with no response content can use 204. If the requested order is not found or disclosed, 404 fits that outcome. If a write conflicts with the current state, consider the declared 409 outcome. Keep credential problems separate from refusal: 401 means valid authentication credentials are lacking, whereas 403 denotes refusal to fulfill. Now compare unexpected failure with temporary inability to handle requests: those correspond to 500 and 503 respectively. First choose the outcome class, then the particular meaning within it. This avoids choosing every rejection as a generic 400 or every operational problem as the same server error. The table is about HTTP meaning, not a promise that a particular framework declaration will automatically produce each status.',
'Client errors and server errors convey different outcome classes. A no-content success is 204, not a promise of a response body.', 'LOW:data_table_demo')
add(14,
'Incoming order data needs a declared shape. Model validation turns compatible input into a model or explains why the input cannot satisfy that shape; it is not merely a test that the original values already had exact types.',
'Imagine an OrderInput model with a required quantity: int field and a note field that has a declared default. Construct it from data with quantity supplied and note omitted. The missing note receives its default, while quantity must be present because it has no default. Try a compatible value that can be converted to the declared quantity type, then try one that cannot satisfy that type. The former illustrates conversion during validation; the latter raises ValidationError with field details. Compare those outcomes with omitting quantity entirely. All three cases answer different questions: was the field supplied, can its value satisfy the type, and what should omission produce when a default exists? Read the resulting model as validated output, not as proof that every input value started with the same type as the corresponding field.',
'Default validation can convert compatible values. An integer in the resulting model does not prove that the original input was already an integer.')
add(15,
'A teammate needs to reconstruct the backend environment without guessing what you installed locally. The dependency declaration becomes part of the project\'s build contract.',
'Inspect installed versions with python -m pip freeze and save the output as requirements.txt for the declaration. Commit that declaration, then use python -m pip install -r requirements.txt in the environment being reconstructed. The important comparison is between the declared packages and the packages obtained for the build, not between two developers\' memories of installation steps. A package that happens to be installed in your working environment is not a reliable substitute for a committed declaration. Think through a handoff: the teammate has the project and its declared dependencies, but not your local environment. The build stage must obtain those dependencies from the declaration. This exercise concerns reconstructing the installed dependency set; do not quietly broaden it into a claim that every external condition of a service is reproduced by freeze alone.',
'A successful run on one manually prepared environment is not a dependency declaration. Reconstruct from the committed declaration, not accidental local installations.', 'HIGH:screen_demo')
add(16,
'As an orders service grows beyond one module, names should show which package owns each module. Running and importing code in that package context makes the qualified identity explicit.',
'Imagine a regular service package containing __init__.py and a module called orders. Its qualified module name is service.orders. An absolute import names the package explicitly; a relative import uses leading dots whose meaning comes from the current package. Run the module in package context with python -m service.orders. In that execution its __name__ is __main__. Trace the same module in two roles: as a module referred to by its qualified package name, and as the module selected to run with -m. The distinction helps you read imports without treating each file as an unrelated top-level script. When choosing an import, ask what package context the leading dots refer to rather than counting directory separators as if that alone defined import meaning.',
'Relative imports depend on the current package. A file\'s location alone is not a substitute for understanding its package context.', 'MEDIUM:annotated_code')
add(17,
'An order rule should hold even when a database write comes from somewhere other than the API. Database constraints enforce row integrity at the stored-data boundary.',
'Consider hypothetical orders with an identity, a customer reference and a quantity. A PRIMARY KEY supplies unique non-null row identity and may use multiple columns. A FOREIGN KEY requires non-null references to match referenced rows. For a quantity that must be present and satisfy a row condition, combine NOT NULL with CHECK rather than asking CHECK alone to reject absence. A check expression that is null passes, so the two declarations cover different cases. For an external reference that should be unique, UNIQUE rejects duplicate combinations, but PostgreSQL treats nulls as distinct by default. If the requirement concerns null duplicates too, account for NULLS NOT DISTINCT. Work through one violating row at a time: null required value, duplicate identity, unmatched reference and failed condition. This identifies which constraint carries which rule instead of assuming application validation is the only place integrity lives.',
'CHECK does not replace NOT NULL: true or null passes a check. PostgreSQL UNIQUE also treats nulls as distinct by default.', 'HIGH:data_table_demo')
add(18,
'A shared resource should be initialized before the service takes requests and released when request handling is finished. Its ownership belongs to the application lifetime, not to each individual handler.',
'Trace the lifespan as a boundary around the request-handling period. In the @asynccontextmanager async function, acquisition happens before yield. The application then serves requests using the acquired shared resource. Cleanup after yield belongs to shutdown after the application finishes handling requests. Pass that context to FastAPI(lifespan=lifespan). In a hypothetical service with a shared external client, draw one acquisition before the first request rather than one inside every handler invocation. Then draw one release after the request-handling lifetime. This makes the resource\'s ownership visible: requests use it, but lifespan owns its startup and cleanup. The reason to share here is the brief\'s explicit goal of avoiding repeated startup work; do not treat the pattern as permission to share every kind of mutable object across requests.',
'Application-lifetime ownership is not per-request acquisition. Use lifespan for a resource intended to be acquired once and borrowed by handlers.', 'HIGH:whiteboard_drawing')
add(19,
'An order may be associated with a customer. The database association and the Python navigation between the two objects are related declarations, but they are not the same declaration.',
'Picture a customer identifier column on Order that uses mapped_column(ForeignKey("customers.id")). That column represents the reference stored in the database. A relationship attribute represents navigation to the related Customer object. On the other side, a customer\'s orders collection can navigate to associated orders. If both relationship attributes use back_populates to name each other, they establish bidirectional in-Python association behavior. Draw the stored foreign-key column separately from the two navigation arrows. This helps you identify which part describes persisted association and which part describes how mapped objects refer to one another. Read the names in back_populates carefully: each names the counterpart relationship attribute, not merely the table name. Keep attention on association mapping without assuming that declaring navigation determines every later loading or query behavior.',
'A relationship attribute does not replace the database foreign-key column. Object navigation and the stored association are declared alongside one another.')
add(20,
'An order quantity being an integer is not enough if the service only accepts quantities in a particular range. Constraints narrow the values admitted by a typed field.',
'For a hypothetical quantity accepted from 1 through 20, inclusive, choose ge=1 and le=20. Compare the boundary values 1 and 20 with 0 and 21. The former meet the range, while the latter do not. If the rule instead excludes a boundary, use gt or lt for that side. A multiple_of rule adds divisibility rather than changing the meaning of the bounds. For an order reference string, min_length and max_length bound length, while pattern expresses a regular-expression match. Put the Field declaration on the typed model attribute so construction applies these constraints. Work from the business rule to the declaration and then back through boundary examples; this is more precise than choosing a plausible-looking number without checking whether the boundary is inclusive.',
'An exclusive bound and an inclusive bound are not interchangeable. Check the exact boundary value when choosing gt versus ge or lt versus le.')
add(21,
'An order body can contain a delivery address and several line items. Rather than leaving the inner objects undescribed, compose smaller models into the enclosing model.',
'Let an address be a Submodel used by the order\'s address field, and line items be a list of another submodel. The address annotation composes one nested model; list[Submodel] composes validation for corresponding collection elements. Imagine the second line item has a value that cannot satisfy its declared field type. The error path can name the enclosing collection, the element index and the failing field. Follow that path inward instead of looking only at the outer order fields. Then compare a bad outer field with a bad nested field: both belong to validation of the enclosing model, but their locations identify different parts of the input. This gives structured bodies an explicit tree of local contracts instead of one large object whose inner data has no declared shape.',
'An enclosing model does not make an invalid nested value acceptable. The submodel\'s validation participates in the enclosing validation.')
add(22,
'An orders API should make item identity and operation meaning clear. The URL identifies what the client is addressing; the HTTP method contributes what is requested.',
'Use /orders for the collection and /orders/{order_id} for an identified item. Read GET on the item as retrieval and PUT on that same item as target replacement; the path alone does not distinguish those operations. POST on the collection can express collection-specific creation processing, while DELETE on the item expresses removal. For a successful creation contract, 201 can be paired with a Location identifying the created item, such as /orders/42. For an absent item, the contract can use 404. Walk through the same identifier with several methods so that resource identity remains stable while requested semantics change. Designing the contract this way also lets you explain each operation without inventing an action word in the path as its sole source of meaning.',
'A path is not a complete operation definition. The method supplies semantics alongside resource identity.', 'HIGH:whiteboard_drawing')
add(23,
'A FastAPI service may retrieve and replace an order at the same path. Registration must distinguish their HTTP methods and also avoid ambiguous route ordering.',
'Register retrieval with @app.get and replacement with @app.put on the intended item path. They are different operations even though their path text matches. Now compare /orders/current with /orders/{order_id}. The fixed current route should precede a conflicting parameterized route, because routes are evaluated in order and the parameterized route can capture that path. Trace a request by asking which method-and-path operation is registered, then which route matches first. This separates two concerns: distinct methods on the same path do not collapse into one operation, while conflicting paths require deliberate order. Read the registration list as behavior the application will evaluate, rather than assuming fixed paths are automatically preferred regardless of where they were declared.',
'Do not assume the framework always chooses a fixed route over an earlier conflicting parameterized route. Declaration order matters.')
add(24,
'A deployment supplies configuration from outside the application. Treating those values as already trustworthy skips the point where missing or invalid settings should be detected.',
'Define a Settings class inheriting BaseSettings, with typed fields for the service\'s required configuration. Construct Settings() to read environment variables and validate them. Try one missing required setting and one value that cannot satisfy its declared type; both should fail validation rather than becoming unchecked application inputs. If a dotenv file is part of the setup, set model_config with SettingsConfigDict(env_file=".env"). When the same setting is supplied in the environment and the dotenv file, the environment value takes precedence. For an exercise, put different synthetic values in those two places and identify which one construction should use. This makes configuration source, priority and validation visible instead of relying on whatever value a developer happened to see in a local file.',
'A dotenv value does not override an environment variable. Missing required configuration and invalid values are validation failures, not silent valid settings.')
add(25,
'An orders handler needs data from an external service. The outbound call is asynchronous, but the client that owns connections also needs a clear lifetime.',
'Use an httpx.AsyncClient for the outbound request and await its request method. Imagine several sequential lookups through the same owning scope: reusing that client lets the calls benefit from connection pooling rather than constructing another client inside a hot loop. At the end of the owning scope, close it using its async context manager or await client.aclose(). Trace ownership separately from each call: the scope creates the client, calls borrow it, and the scope closes it. That trace is useful whether the scope covers several operations or an application-lifetime resource. Keep the example focused on making and closing asynchronous requests; connection ownership does not by itself specify timeout policy or what a remote failure should mean to the local API.',
'An awaited request does not replace client cleanup. Reuse a scoped client where appropriate and close it at the end of that scope.')
add(26,
'A booking order can have individually valid start and end values that form an invalid pair. A domain invariant checks the relationship after ordinary field validation.',
'For a hypothetical rule that end must be later than start, supply two values that each satisfy its field type but put end before start. An after-model validator sees the already-validated fields and raises ValueError for that invariant failure; model construction raises ValidationError. Supply a consistent pair next and return self from the successful validator. Compare this with a single-field custom check: an after-field validator uses a classmethod and returns the accepted validated value. Keep the two roles distinct. The pairwise rule is not adequately demonstrated by sending a value that already fails its own field type, because that would not isolate the cross-field invariant. Choose test data that passes the local field checks and fails only the relationship you are trying to teach.',
'A cross-field rule is not proved by an input that already fails individual field validation. Isolate the invariant with individually valid values.')
add(27,
'An absent order is an expected API rejection, not a normal successful result containing an exception object. HTTPException expresses a deliberate HTTP outcome.',
'When the operation cannot find the requested order under its contract, raise HTTPException with the intended status and a client-facing detail. Raising stops the operation and produces the rejection; returning the exception object is not the same instruction. In FastAPI\'s default response shape, the supplied detail appears under the JSON key detail. If the rejection requires response metadata, provide headers on the exception, such as an authentication challenge when that outcome needs one. Trace the branch at which the exception is raised: normal operation work stops there, and the response is the deliberate HTTP rejection. Keep detail suitable for the client rather than using this mechanism as a reason to disclose internal diagnostic information.',
'Raise HTTPException rather than returning it. The default body puts the supplied information under detail, not under an arbitrary invented key.')
add(28,
'A handler calls an I/O library. Choosing async def is only appropriate when the work can cooperate through awaitable operations; ordinary blocking calls do not become asynchronous because of the handler\'s spelling.',
'Compare two hypothetical shipping clients: one offers awaitable I/O and the other offers blocking I/O. For the awaitable library, use async def and await its operations. For the blocking library, a normal def handler runs in FastAPI\'s external thread pool. Now move a blocking call into an ordinary helper called directly from an async def handler. FastAPI does not automatically move that helper to the thread pool, so the blocking call can block the event loop. Trace execution through the handler into the utility rather than inspecting only the route declaration. The decisive question is what boundary executes the I/O operation, not whether a helper has been placed in a separate file or given a name that sounds asynchronous.',
'FastAPI\'s handling of normal def path operations does not apply automatically to ordinary utility calls made directly inside an async handler.', 'HIGH:whiteboard_drawing')
add(29,
'Order operations belong together, but the main application still needs to assemble them. APIRouter separates feature registration from application assembly.',
'Create an orders router and register the feature\'s operations on it. In the application assembly module, import that router and include it with app.include_router(router). If an operation has a local /orders path and inclusion adds a /api prefix, reason about the included path as /api/orders. Inclusion can also supply tags, dependencies and responses. Keep those assembly choices visible at the include point rather than assuming they came from the feature\'s local path text. Follow the feature from router registration to inclusion so you can explain how it becomes part of the application. The router organizes operations; merely having the feature module present is not the assembly step.',
'Defining a router is not the same as including it in the application. Trace include_router and any prefix it adds.')
add(30,
'A request for one order can also carry a query setting. FastAPI binds these values according to the path and parameter declarations rather than treating every input as the same source.',
'For /orders/{order_id}, declare order_id: int in the handler to bind and convert the path value. A scalar parameter named limit that is not in the path is a query parameter. If limit has a default, it may be omitted; without a default, it is required. Compare a supplied string that can convert to the declared type with an order_id that cannot: the invalid path value produces a validation error instead of reaching the handler as the original unconverted string. Draw one arrow from the path placeholder and another from the query string to their declared arguments. This keeps source binding, supplied-value conversion and omission behavior as three visible parts of the operation contract.',
'A parameter\'s name alone does not make it a path input. It must occur in the path declaration; ordinary scalar inputs outside the path bind from the query.')
add(31,
'An internal order object may contain fields the client must not receive. The public response model is an explicit output contract, not a request to serialize every available attribute.',
'Define a PublicOrder model containing only intended public fields, then set response_model=PublicOrder on the operation. Imagine the internal value also contains a private processing note: leaving that field out of the public model prevents it from being returned merely because it exists internally. Response processing validates, serializes and filters according to that public model. If the handler also has a return annotation, response_model takes priority for FastAPI\'s processing. Now compare returning a Response object directly: that bypasses automatic model conversion and filtering, so the application must take responsibility for its content. This is an important boundary to trace when checking disclosure: identify the return form, then identify which output-processing path it actually uses.',
'A response model is not an automatic safety net for a directly returned Response object. That path bypasses its conversion and filtering.')
add(32,
'Several order operations need a value supplied by reusable logic. A dependency makes that needed input part of the handler\'s declaration rather than an invisible global lookup.',
'Declare an input as Annotated[Type, Depends(provider)]. Read that as an instruction for FastAPI to execute provider, resolve its inputs and supply its returned value to the handler. The declaration passes the provider callable, not the result of calling provider in advance. Trace the boundary in that order: provider definition, dependency declaration, request-time resolution, supplied handler argument. Another operation can declare the same provider for the same reusable input-provision logic. This makes the needed input visible where the handler is read. Keep attention on what the dependency supplies and how it is declared; this concept does not require treating all service logic as dependencies or inventing an application-wide hidden value store.',
'Depends(provider) passes a callable. Calling provider in the declaration would not express the same input-resolution instruction.')
add(33,
'An outbound lookup should not wait without a bound. But a chunk-level I/O timeout and a total elapsed-time budget answer different questions.',
'For a hypothetical shipping lookup, configure a finite HTTPX timeout. Connect, read, write and pool waits have distinct limits, and read or write timeouts apply to waiting for individual chunks. A response that keeps supplying chunks within the read limit does not make that limit a whole-request elapsed deadline. If the local contract also requires the asynchronous work to finish within a total budget, wrap it in async with asyncio.timeout(seconds). Its cancellation is converted to TimeoutError outside that context. Compare a connection wait, a stalled response chunk and work exceeding the total elapsed budget, identifying the boundary for each. This separates the HTTPX timeout exceptions from the total-budget exception without assuming that one number attached to the client guarantees all elapsed work has the same bound.',
'HTTPX read and write timeouts are not whole-request elapsed deadlines. Also, timeout=None disables timeout enforcement.', 'HIGH:whiteboard_drawing')
add(34,
'An order handler currently parses HTTP input, decides a business rule and accesses storage in one place. Moving the same mixture into three files would not by itself separate responsibilities.',
'Keep HTTP adaptation in the handler, move the business decision to a plain callable with explicit inputs, and call a persistence adapter for stored state. A dependency can extract reusable input provisioning as a declared parameter. Trace an order rule through those roles: the handler adapts the request, the business callable decides, and the adapter accesses storage. The boundary is what each part does, not the number of modules.',
'Separate files alone are not separate concerns. Responsibilities must be separated so one can change without embedding the others.', 'LOW:whiteboard_drawing')
add(35,
'An operation can send its response before some small follow-up work runs. BackgroundTasks provides an in-process scheduling boundary, not a durable delivery guarantee.',
'Declare a BackgroundTasks parameter and add a callable with background_tasks.add_task(callable, *args, **kwargs). In a hypothetical order workflow, the response is sent and then an in-process follow-up task runs. The callable can be normal def or async def; FastAPI handles either form. Trace a process failure after the response: the task is part of that process and its survival or delivery cannot be guaranteed. Therefore the response and the follow-up effect should not be described as if task scheduling proved durable completion. Keep this example to work whose contract fits that limitation. The important decision is not simply whether the follow-up is convenient to defer, but whether its required outcome is compatible with the process-local execution boundary.',
'Adding a background task does not prove that it survives process failure or delivers its effect. In-process scheduling is not durable completion.', 'HIGH:whiteboard_drawing')
add(36,
'A handler may need a provider whose own result depends on another provider. Dependencies compose into a graph, so the prerequisites must be supplied before their consumer can run.',
'Imagine an order-options provider depends on a pagination provider. The pagination provider declares typed query inputs and returns its result. FastAPI extracts those query inputs, resolves pagination, and supplies that value to order-options before calling it. Only then can the consuming operation receive the order-options result. Draw arrows from needed input toward the consumer, then trace execution from the request inputs through the prerequisite providers toward the handler. This avoids reading nested declarations as if the outer provider ran first and obtained prerequisites afterward. Each provider can expose its own needed inputs explicitly; composition is still input provisioning rather than a hidden sequence of global mutations.',
'A provider does not run before its unresolved prerequisite inputs are available. Resolve the graph from prerequisites to consumers.', 'HIGH:whiteboard_drawing')
add(37,
'Two maintained middleware components wrap a service. To reason about their order, picture nested wrappers rather than a flat list executed the same way in both directions.',
'Call the first addition A and the second addition B. B is the outermost wrapper because it was added last. The request travels B, then A, then the handler. The response travels back through A and finally B. Draw the handler inside A inside B and trace arrows inward and outward; the reversal becomes visible. Configure each component through app.add_middleware with its documented options rather than implementing its protocol yourself. When checking a supplied middleware stack, record addition order before tracing traversal. The same configuration list explains both directions, but the sequence differs because the response leaves the nested application boundary. A list labeled merely "middleware order" without a direction can obscure that distinction.',
'The last addition sees requests first and responses last. Request traversal and response traversal are not identical sequences.', 'HIGH:whiteboard_drawing')
add(38,
'A development server with automatic reload is convenient locally, but a production process needs deployment choices suited to stable serving.',
'In uvicorn main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000, main names the module and app its application object. Verify that import string against the project structure. Leave --reload for development rather than enabling it in production. If the deployment chooses multiple worker processes, use --workers with the supplied worker count; it is mutually exclusive with --reload. Do not use reload as a substitute for that process choice.',
'Reload is not production process management. Worker count belongs to the deployment decision, and workers cannot be combined with reload.', 'MEDIUM:screen_demo')
add(39,
'An order-creation request contains a structured JSON object, while its URL may also identify a parent resource and supply a scalar query setting. Those inputs can coexist without sharing one binding source.',
'Define an OrderInput BaseModel and annotate the body parameter with it. FastAPI reads the JSON body, converts compatible values, validates the model and passes a model instance to the handler. Compare this with a path-declared customer identifier and an ordinary scalar query option: those bind from the path and generally from the query, respectively. Draw the body as one structured input rather than scattering its fields into assumed query parameters. Then trace a request from body bytes to validated model to handler argument. The model instance is the result of body binding and validation, not simply the untouched JSON data. Keeping source and declared shape together lets you explain where each part of the operation\'s input comes from.',
'A structured model parameter binds from the body; it is not the same input declaration as an ordinary scalar query parameter.')

